1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:08,120
In the early 1900s, Austrian scientist Karl von Frisch discovered the “waggle dance” of honey bees.
20世纪初，奥地利科学家卡尔·冯·弗里施发现了蜜蜂的“摇摆舞”。

2
00:00:08,920 --> 00:00:13,880
They dance to communicate where to find food, showing an advanced form of communication.
它们通过跳舞来传达去哪里能找到食物，这展现出一种高级的交流方式。

3
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:18,570
His discovery suggested that bees seem to “think” much like humans.
他的发现表明，蜜蜂“思考”起来似乎和人类很像。

4
00:00:19,370 --> 00:00:24,710
Bees can count up to four and even use knowledge learned in one situation in new ones.
蜜蜂能数到四，甚至能把在一种情境中学到的知识用到新的情境中。

5
00:00:25,510 --> 00:00:33,370
Another amazing ability they have is similar to that of a few mammals (like dolphins, rats, and some monkeys)—metacognition.
它们还有一种惊人的能力，与少数几种哺乳动物（比如海豚、老鼠和一些猴子）的能力相似，那就是元认知。

6
00:00:34,170 --> 00:00:38,240
Studying bees’ brains is challenging, but scientists have found ways.
研究蜜蜂的大脑很有挑战性，但科学家们已经找到了办法。

7
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:43,500
One such scientist is Clint Perry from Queen Mary University of London.
伦敦玛丽女王大学的克林特·佩里就是这样一位科学家。

8
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:50,080
He designed experiments where bees chose between two spots, one above and one below a black bar.
他设计了这样一些实验：蜜蜂在两个位置之间做选择，一个位于一根黑色横条的上方，另一个位于横条的下方。

9
00:00:50,880 --> 00:00:56,290
If they landed above, they got a sweet drink; if below, a terrible one.
如果落在上方，它们就会得到甜的饮料；如果落在下方，得到的就是难喝的饮料。

10
00:00:57,090 --> 00:01:02,990
Perry made the test easier or harder by moving the landing spots further from or closer to the black bar.
通过把降落点移得离黑色横条更远或更近，佩里让测试变得更容易或更难。

11
00:01:03,790 --> 00:01:10,590
Perry found that bees avoided difficult tests and tried easy ones, suggesting they can judge their chances of “passing”.
佩里发现，蜜蜂会避开难的测试，去尝试容易的测试，这表明它们能判断自己“通过”的可能性。

12
00:01:11,390 --> 00:01:19,410
This ability to make decisions in advance according to their chances of success is similar to how humans choose whether to take a test.
这种根据自己成功的可能性提前做出决定的能力，与人类选择是否参加考试的方式相似。

13
00:01:20,210 --> 00:01:27,500
However, Perry admits we can’t be sure if bees are truly judging by their own knowledge or just avoiding an unpleasant taste.
不过，佩里承认，我们无法确定蜜蜂是真的在根据自己的知识作判断，还是只是在避开令人不快的味道。

14
00:01:28,300 --> 00:01:34,870
Although there is much uncertainty, the discovery of metacognition in bees opens up new possibilities.
尽管还有很多不确定之处，但在蜜蜂身上发现元认知，开启了新的可能。

15
00:01:35,670 --> 00:01:38,960
It suggests that bees’ tiny brains may be quite capable.
这表明，蜜蜂小小的大脑可能具有相当强的能力。

16
00:01:39,760 --> 00:01:45,720
It could be that skills such as simple math and navigation take far less actual brainpower than we thought.
像简单的数学和导航这样的技能，实际需要的脑力有可能比我们原以为的少得多。

17
00:01:46,520 --> 00:01:54,930
While bees aren’t as smart as mammals in memory and language, studying their brains could help us understand how our own brains process information.
虽然蜜蜂在记忆和语言方面不如哺乳动物聪明，但研究它们的大脑可能会帮助我们了解自己的大脑是如何处理信息的。

18
00:01:55,730 --> 00:01:59,690
We may also have to rethink how we view the rest of the insect world.
我们或许还得重新思考自己看待昆虫世界其他成员的方式。

19
00:02:00,490 --> 00:02:05,750
There are an awful lot of small insects flying around in the garden and crawling underground.
有非常多的小昆虫在花园里飞来飞去，在地下爬来爬去。

20
00:02:06,550 --> 00:02:09,490
Who knows what those tiny minds might be capable of?
谁知道那些小小的头脑可能会有什么样的本事呢？
